<article class="env-lemma" id="0GZQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GZQ">Lemma <span data-tag="0GZQ">21.43.3</span>.</a> In the situation above, suppose that $M$ is an object of $\mathit{QC}(\mathcal{O})$ and $b \in \mathbf{Z}$ such that $H^ i(M) = 0$ for all $i > b$. Then $H^ b(M)$ is a quasi-coherent module on $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ in the sense of Modules on Sites, Definition <a href="/tag/03DL" data-tag="03DL">18.23.1</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Modules on Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/0GZN" data-tag="0GZN">18.24.3</a> it suffices to show that for every morphism $U \to V$ of $\mathcal{C}$ the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  H^ p(M)(V) \otimes _{\mathcal{O}(V)} \mathcal{O}(U) \to H^ b(M)(U)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is an isomorphism. We are given that the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R\Gamma (V, M) \otimes _{\mathcal{O}(V)}^\mathbf {L} \mathcal{O}(U) \to R\Gamma (U, M)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is an isomorphism. Thus the result by the Tor spectral sequence for example. Details omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>